  "articleBody": "Installing a wheelchair ramp might seem straightforward, but there are definitely some common missteps that can turn a helpful addition into a real headache. One of the biggest mistakes is a poor site assessment. You really need to accurately measure the vertical rise and ensure you have enough horizontal space for a gentle slope. Many folks underestimate this, leading to ramps that are too steep and unsafe. Another frequent issue is ignoring local building codes and permit requirements. Depending on its size and permanence, your ramp might need a permit, and skipping this step can lead to fines or having to tear it down. Also, don't forget about crucial safety features like edge protection, proper handrails for longer ramps, and a non-slip surface; these aren't just extras, they're essential for preventing accidents. Trying to DIY a complex ramp without experience can often result in a structure that isn't stable or doesn't meet safety standards. When in doubt, especially for high-rise or custom layouts, it's always best to bring in experienced professionals like HH Remodeling LLC. We can make sure your ramp is safe, compliant, and perfectly suited to your needs.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
